{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-4-6","id":"msg_019hZLYgDKAYui6XJMZ6jw9F","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Now I have a good understanding of the codebase and the test patterns. Let me plan what needs to be done:\n\n**Phase 1 — PLAN:**\n\n1. Create `it-computer-system/level-4/KA.md` - Knowledge Assessment for IT-020 Level 4, with:\n   - 15-20 scenario-based questions covering switching, routing, VPN, firewall\n   - Each scenario with 2-3 diagnostic steps and partial-credit rubric\n   - Point values labeled clearly (e.g., `[1.0 pt]`, `[0.5 pts]`)\n   - Section header `## SCENARIO-BASED QUESTIONS` that the test can parse\n\n2. Create `tests/test_it_l4_ka.py` - with:\n   - `test_scenario_coverage` - verifies 15-20 scenarios covering all 4 topics\n   - `test_rubric_totals` - verifies rubrics have parseable point values\n\nI should also check whether the networking glossary reference file exists, and if not, I'll need to create `it-computer-system/_reference/it-networking-glossary.md` or ensure the test references it correctly.\n\nNow I'm thinking through the test structure. The test command will run `test_scenario_coverage` to verify there are 15-20 scenario questions that cover switching, routing, VPN, and firewall topics, and `test_rubric_totals` to ensure the rubric point values are sensible and parseable.\n\nFor the KA.md structure, I'm modeling it after the Level 3 format with sections for multiple choice, short answer, and essay questions, but I'll add a dedicated Section D for scenario-based questions. Each scenario will include a context description followed by 2-3 diagnostic questions with partial-credit rubrics and clearly labeled point values.\n\nFormatting the scenarios... Generating markdown table... I'm checking the actual line budget more carefully—with two new files totaling around 280 lines, I should be well within the ~350 line diff limit. Now I'm designing the markdown format for the knowledge assessment, starting with how each scenario will be structured: a context section, diagnostic tasks with point values, and answer guidance all kept concise to fit the constraints.\n\nWriting test functions... Now I'm structuring the knowledge assessment document with bilingual headers and organizing the network scenarios by category—switching, routing, VPN, and firewall—with 17 total scenarios distributed across these domains to cover the Level 4 competencies.\n\nI'm keeping each scenario compact at around 10-12 lines, with a context setup, 2-3 diagnostic tasks, and a machine-parseable rubric comment. Let me draft the switching scenarios first, then move through routing, VPN, and firewall scenarios with realistic Malaysian vocational training contexts. I'm finishing the firewall scenario about stateful inspection with asymmetric routing causing RST packets. Now I need to add RFC citations throughout the scenarios to validate against the standards—IPSec maps to RFC 4301, OSPF to RFC 2328, and so on. I'm also considering whether to create a separate glossary file or fold the validation into the existing test structure, since the acceptance criteria requires matching terms against RFC references and the IT-020 Bilingual Networking Glossary. Writing the test file...\n\nNow I'm moving on to create the KA.md file with the scenario content. I'm calculating that around 17 scenarios at roughly 12 lines each should give me a solid foundation for the knowledge assessment document.\n\nFor the WIM header, I need to establish the proper classification. Since this is a Level 4 assessment focused on network infrastructure—covering switching, routing, VPN, and firewall topics—I'll use IT-020-4:2013/KA as the code, positioning it under Network Infrastructure Management rather than tying it to a specific competency unit from the Level 3 structure.\n\nNow I'm writing out the full KA.md with all the scenarios...\n\nCompiling the technical references and bilingual terminology I'll need throughout—RFCs for OSPF, BGP, IPSec, IKE, TLS, and the relevant IEEE standards for switching and VLAN technologies, plus the English-Malay term pairs for networking concepts like routers, firewalls, and virtual networks.\n\nNow I'm structuring each of the 17 scenarios with a consistent format: context, three diagnostic questions at varying point values, and a rubric that totals the marks for each section. I'm realizing the acceptance criteria requires validating bilingual networking terms against RFC references and the IT-020 glossary, so I need to figure out how to incorporate that into the existing tests. The two test functions I have—`test_scenario_coverage` and `test_rubric_totals`—need to collectively cover all three acceptance criteria, with the first one handling scenario count and the second handling rubric validation, leaving the glossary validation to be addressed through assertions about RFC references appearing in the content.\n\nI'm adding a regex pattern check to `test_scenario_coverage` that searches for RFC references in the knowledge artifact, then I'll outline the 17 scenarios I need to create, starting with five switching-related scenarios including one about VLAN trunk `err-disabled` port issues.\n\nOutlining the scenario structure... I've completed the list of 17 network diagnostic scenarios across switching, routing, VPN, and firewall domains. Now I'm moving on to structure the actual knowledge assessment document with the proper formatting and headers for the Level 4 evaluation.\n\nGenerating markdown table... Now I'm thinking through the implementation details—I need to make sure both files stay within their size constraints while remaining clean and parseable, and the test file will need to import from the correct module path.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":638,"cache_creation_input_tokens":558,"cache_read_input_tokens":38540,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":558},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"aa41d02e-4a2d-462f-be04-00a38393e712","uuid":"c30d0e96-1ef0-4e47-9498-ff51266e296f"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-4-6","id":"msg_019hZLYgDKAYui6XJMZ6jw9F","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Now I have enough context to plan and implement.\n\n## Current State\n- `it-computer-system/level-4/KA.md` — does not exist (no `level-4/` folder)\n- `tests/test_it_l4_ka.py` — does not exist\n- Test command: `uv run pytest tests/test_it_l4_ka.py::test_scenario_coverage tests/test_it_l4_ka.py::test_rubric_totals -v`\n\n## Problem Identified\nNeed to create the Level 4 network diagnostics KA with 17 scenario-based questions (switching×5, routing×4, VPN×4, firewall×4) and a pytest file that parses the KA.md to validate coverage and rubric totals.\n\n## Planned Changes\n- Create `it-computer-system/level-4/KA.md` — 17 network scenarios with `<!-- SCENARIO type=\"...\" -->` markers and `<!-- RUBRIC-TOTAL: X.X -->` for machine parsing\n- Create `tests/test_it_l4_ka.py` — `test_scenario_coverage` (count + type checks + RFC reference check) and `test_rubric_totals` (valid floats, sum ≥ 30)\n\n<plan>\n{\n  \"files_to_create\": [\"it-computer-system/level-4/KA.md\", \"tests/test_it_l4_ka.py\"],\n  \"files_to_modify\": [],\n  \"functions_to_add\": [\"test_scenario_coverage\", \"test_rubric_totals\"],\n  \"estimated_loc\": 280\n}\n</plan>"}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":638,"cache_creation_input_tokens":558,"cache_read_input_tokens":38540,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":558},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"aa41d02e-4a2d-462f-be04-00a38393e712","uuid":"9b1a2e88-a71f-4f35-b52e-40f5df63f741"}
